WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
JENNY BROWN: Welcome to Cloud
Security Basics, a series where

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.730
we explain the ins and outs
of securing your application

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.080
on Google Cloud.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.070
Sound fun?

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.270
Then stick around,
because in this episode

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:13.380
you'll learn good practices
for securing your application

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.430
with Cloud IAM.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.740
[SIRENS]

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.130
[STATIC]

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.070
[DIAL UP MODEM SOUND]

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.470
BRAD ACTOR: It's been a
while, Cloud Detective.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.620
I see you understand the
flexibility and capabilities

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:39.910
of the cloud.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.940
As you know, it makes it easier
to work faster and collaborate

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:44.810
more effectively.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.920
But it also introduces new
responsibilities and security

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.160
risks.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.100
And sometimes, those threats
are not on the outside.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.560
Accounts can get hacked,
credentials leaked,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.500
and employees disgruntled.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.830
Whether accidental or
willful, internal users

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.349
represent the biggest threat
to your organization's cloud

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:05.940
security.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.420
For your sake, Detective, I hope
you can keep the wrong people

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.010
from accessing the wrong
resources at the wrong time.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.380
JENNY BROWN: OK,
here's what we know.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.410
Companies need to make sure
that only the right people can

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:23.880
access only the right resources
at only the right time.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.060
One way to keep bad
actors out would

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.080
be to cut access for everyone
and have a few trusted people

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.210
dole out access manually.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.360
But keeping productivity
high and getting the most out

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.870
of your cloud platform depends
on employees having access

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:42.470
to the resources and assets
they need with the least

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:44.510
amount of friction or delay.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.630
And while just manually
allowing access every time

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.240
a person or application
needs resources

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.800
might work for a very, very
small organization, that

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.860
would quickly grow
unwieldy as the teams grow

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.820
or begin to work remotely.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.530
So in order for our organization
to grow and function,

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.960
we need a better way
to manage access.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.710
Luckily, Google Cloud provides
a lot of tools and services

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:11.180
to protect its users.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.510
And this seems like a good time
to use Google Cloud's Identity

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.740
and Access Management, actually.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.670
So IAM is Google
Cloud's unified system

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:23.690
for managing resource access,
assigning user permissions,

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.840
and controlling cloud
application service use.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.240
It unifies all
access and control

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.150
and centralizes
it into one place.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:35.810
Resources are the fundamental,
lowest-level components

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.330
that make up all
Google Cloud Services.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:41.840
So some examples would
include Compute Engine VMs,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.620
Pub/Sub topics, Cloud Storage
buckets, and App Engine

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.820
instances.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:50.300
Cloud IAM lets organizations
keep track of everything

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.640
going on in their
Google Cloud environment

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.120
and prevent unauthorized
resource access.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.250
Let's research more
to know how I can

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.350
use this to stop those threats.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.750
Cloud IAM's access
management system

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.360
breaks down into
a few basic parts.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:11.310
There's members, resources,
permissions, roles, policies,

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:12.900
and groups.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.640
Members, or identities, can be
a Google account for end users,

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.570
a service account used to manage
apps and virtual machines,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:25.500
a Google Group, or a Google
Workspace Cloud Identity domain

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.720
able to access a resource.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.830
The member's identity is usually
the associated email address,

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:36.070
service account, or
group or domain name.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:39.610
Resources, these are the
fundamental components

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.970
that make up all Google
Cloud services like VMs,

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:44.930
storage buckets, and more.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:49.240
The organizations, folders, and
projects used to organize them

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.970
are also considered resources.

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:54.760
And permissions, which
are very fine-grained

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.180
access control on a
particular resource.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.020
Roles.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.090
A role is a collection
of permissions,

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.490
and permissions
determine what operations

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.590
are allowed on a resource.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.720
When you grant a
role to a member,

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.020
you grant them all the
permissions the role contains.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.660
Google Cloud has
predefined, built-in roles,

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.810
and you can also
create custom roles.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:21.459
Policies, which bind a set of
Cloud IAM members to a role.

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:24.460
And if you want to define
who has what type of access

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.950
on a resource, you
create a policy

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.590
and attach it to that resource.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:33.160
The policy also has other
information, such as auditing

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.710
configuration, but
we're going to focus

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.540
on identity aspects for now.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:38.470
And groups.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:42.310
Groups are used to help
you manage users at scale.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:45.220
Each member of a Google
Group inherits the identity

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.120
and access management roles
that you granted to that group.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.000
And this means that you can
use a group's membership

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.240
to manage users' roles
instead of granting

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.852
IAM roles to individual users.

00:04:58.852 --> 00:04:59.740
Hmm.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:04.600
So how should I use IAM
to protect my organization

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.210
from inside threats?

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.270
I could start by using
groups from the beginning.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.430
This way, the security
admin wouldn't

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.350
have to manually
change permissions

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:17.500
as employees get
hired, fired, or move

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.800
throughout the organization
because the right policies

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.850
would be assigned to anyone I
move into the correct group.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.190
This would also
make it a lot easier

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.760
to minimize mistakes, because
instead of assigning roles

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.310
and permissions to
a specific employee,

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:36.700
we could assign them to
groups and then add employees

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.510
to the correct groups.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:40.960
This works even if
you already have

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:44.170
an organizational hierarchy
and groups in an existing

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.330
system like Active Directory.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:49.240
And in that case,
you can continuously

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.060
sync that configuration
to Cloud IAM,

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.480
providing a single source of
truth for group membership

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.390
and reducing the
chance of a mistake.

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.820
And once we have
groups, we could also

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.250
make sure to name the
groups descriptively

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:06.430
so that it's obvious to tell at
a glance what each of the roles

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:07.840
has access to.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.760
And this should
significantly reduce

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.310
mistakes that come from
assigning the wrong roles

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.110
to the wrong groups.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:17.170
A good strategy here would
be to make a nested folder

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:19.120
hierarchy apparent with naming.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.510
One general rule would be to
begin with the organization

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:26.680
name and then expand out the
folder tree with the project

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.180
name.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:29.170
That's it.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:33.100
I can use these components to
shore up my system's defenses.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:35.220
Let's see what Brad
Actor thinks about that.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:35.720
[STATIC]

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:36.428
BRAD ACTOR: Argh.

00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:40.390
Detective, it seems like
you've foiled my attempt again.

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:43.840
Somehow, you figured out
that properly using Cloud IAM

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.560
greatly reduces the risk
of security violations.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.230
I'm surprised you even
picked up on using groups

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:52.450
to simplify the process
of managing access

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:56.620
rights for employees that
move around your organization.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.470
But what about the access you've
given to non-human entities

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:02.260
like applications that need to
access your secure resources

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:03.130
to function?

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:05.350
Have you secured those?

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:08.380
For your sake, I hope you
have, because next time,

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.450
I'm not focusing
on your employees,

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:14.067
but instead on your
scripts and applications.

00:07:14.067 --> 00:07:16.150
JENNY BROWN: So there you
have it, another episode

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.130
of Cloud Security Basics.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:21.010
Next episode, we'll focus
on using service accounts

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:24.580
to protect automated access
to your cloud resources.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.400
So stay tuned for the rest
of the Cloud Security Basics

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.130
series, because when
it comes to security,

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.290
you can't let bad actors win.

